From 4ae13b75885f70312fe638609f32c31fe5612c01 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 27 Sep 2026 03:59:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(lighting): propagate increased block light incrementally

Flood-fill new emission into cached light arrays and send only changed sections. Recompute the versioned region when emission falls, opacity changes, or nearby chunk state is incomplete; align light snapshots with waterlogged Java block states.

Resolve Bedrock slab filtering by Java slab type when constructing the
light snapshot table. An isolated official Bedrock Dedicated Server
1.26.51.1 probe, build 51061372 and protocol 2193, measured getSkyLightLevel
and getLightLevel through sealed shafts and tunnels without client joins.
Top and bottom oak and smooth-stone slabs read skylight 14 at the slab
then 13 below; double slabs block both sky and block light. Java combines
half and double slabs under one identifier, so its identifier opacity
zero loses both native distinctions. Half slabs now filter one level
and type=double filters 15.

The same target probe shows top/bottom stairs pass full skylight 15,
water and leaves filter one level, and single slabs pass block light
14 then 13. Preserve those measured scalar rules instead of importing
Java face occlusion. Numeric propagation tests reproduce the native
shaft and tunnel measurements; raw official probe output stays private.

Target Bedrock 1.26.51.1 additionally measures waterlogged stairs,
fences, chains and lanterns filtering one sky-light level where their
dry states pass 15. Waterlogged leaves and half slabs retain filtering
one; custom filters zero, eight and fifteen measure max(primary, one),
not summed absorption. Resolve Java waterlogged states accordingly and
snapshot native secondary water independently of Java state conversion.
This preserves wet custom-carrier filtering when no Java waterlogged
variant exists, without mutating packet palettes or carrier identity.
Adding/removing secondary water invalidates cached light even when the
Java state stays unchanged. Replacement columns discard both state and
water snapshots; incremental block light retains its existing minimum
one-level loss and therefore needs no secondary-water adjustment.

Numeric propagation and palette-coordinate tests cover native filtering,
wet/dry separation, water removal, and immutable snapshots. Native probe
raw data stays private. Script API light queries include time/weather
adjustment, so all propagation comparisons were taken at clear noon.

Retain queued lighting refreshes while the same chunk key has an older worker in flight. A server can replace a column before that worker completes; the completion discards the old column snapshot by identity, so removing the new dirty key would permanently lose its border refresh. Skip pending keys without blocking unrelated updates and remove only unloaded or dispatched keys. A failing-before regression reproduces replacement, independent dispatch, and retry after completion. This fixes an upstream scheduling edge case without establishing the cause of a reported server slowdown.

Match native source emission in the same cached propagation path. An
isolated official BDS 1.26.51.1 sealed tunnel, build 51061372 and protocol
2193, reports zero light next to redstone dust retaining signal 15,
soul campfire air-cell levels 9/8/7, and lit redstone/deepslate ore levels
8/7/6. Extinguished/unlit controls remain dark. Correct source emission
to zero, ten and nine respectively; retain ordinary campfire and furnace
rules confirmed by the same measurements. Keep this in core for both
direct and ViaProxy clients. Regression tests cover chunk-border full and
incremental propagation, cache immutability and source removal. Native
assets and probe output remain private. Rendered image parity remains open.

Bound the lifetime of background lighting jobs through result application or discard, including slow connection event loops, rejected executors and failures. Releasing worker capacity before completion delivery allowed queued callbacks to retain unlimited region snapshots. Keep one permit per job and release it exactly once.

Keep uniform lighting snapshots as one state instead of 4,096 integers. Reuse the emission workspace for block light, and avoid allocating arrays for uniform zero/full light sections. Preserve mixed-section, secondary-water, border and incremental propagation semantics. Stress tests exercise 32,000 submissions with blocked result delivery, abandoned trackers and exception cleanup. Full-height and dense/compact parity tests check the storage bound and output. A private 24-section benchmark produces identical light data with approximately 27 percent less allocation per update; its mostly uniform snapshot uses approximately 96 percent less state-array storage. These measurements do not establish game-wide heap or frame-rate improvement.

Keep at most one queued or running chunk-tracker callback per connection.
A slow event loop previously retained every timer callback and processed
catch-up bursts after chunk conversion or other blocking work completed.
Dirty chunks and light work already remain in their tracker queues, so
repeated timer callbacks need no separate replay.

Release the connection reservation in finally when the callback runs or
is discarded. Recover rejected submissions during channel shutdown and
clean up other submission failures. Identity-specific reservation tokens
prevent an old failed submission from clearing a newer callback's slot.
Keep the existing callback boundary so subsequent dimension-tracker
identity checks still reject stale work.

Five manual-executor regression tests cover 32 bursts of 1,000 redundant
timer submissions, independent connections, a running callback, discarded
work, failed callbacks, rejected executors, and replacement reservations
across inline executor failure. All pass; changed test Checkstyle passes.
